# VulkanPCloud — `pc::`

This system extracts the complete original point-cloud, shadow, indirect
irradiance, and direct-lighting pipeline from `xt9y/vulkan-game`. It owns
surface-probe generation (0.3 spacing), light-derived 3D probe grid,
BVH construction including per-light local trees, bricked point index,
point-cloud trace, GI gather, tile-binned direct light, PBR shading,
tonemapping, and the original full-screen Vulkan presentation. The six
original shader files are kept unmodified in `pcloud/shaders/`.

The public `pc::` API accepts **only** light data, surface collision boxes,
world-space caster triangles, camera parameters, and G-buffer image views.
It does not include `clt/`, `srv/`, `game::`, scene streaming, input, Ozz,
GLFW, or editor types. Hosts are responsible for generating geometry from
their own meshes and animated characters (same as a geometry pass), and
for providing a compatible four-attachment G-buffer. This preserves shader
math and exact scene-to-light geometry when the host provides the same
world-space triangles. The Vulkan backend requires the existing
VulkanWrapper, VulkanGPU, VulkanRender, VulkanBVH, VulkanSpatial, VulkanJobs.

```cpp
pc::configuration shaders{};
pc::presentation_target surface{swapchain_images,swapchain_format,extent};
pc::create(shaders,gbuffer_views,surface); // after device and G-buffer
pc::set_scene(world_ptr);                // shared_ptr<const pc::world>
pc::synchronize_scene();           // only when previous GPU frames are idle
const pc::targets img=pc::images(image_index);
// Schedule pc::cloud, pc::gi, pc::lighting, pc::tonemap, pc::present
// using your renderer's graph barriers. Call pc::updated(image_index)
// after successful submit; pc::release() before Vulkan device shutdown.
```

The `pc::camera_frame` contains world eye position, yaw, pitch, FOV
(vertical radians). `pc::present` accepts an optional callback that draws
custom UI in the final pass, after tonemapping. The game adapter is solely
responsible for converting its scene, skin matrices, and camera into these
generic inputs. To compile the shader files, use `glslangValidator -V` and
pass each output path in `pc::configuration`. No shaders or point-cloud
algorithms need to live in the host's source tree.

The implementation maintains one active Vulkan context. It accepts the
host's presentation images, format and extent explicitly (no global
`vk::swapchain::get()` dependency), and assumes the four G-buffer formats
and material packing match
the bundled GLSL shaders. Adapting to Metal, D3D12, or different G-buffer
contracts requires a new backend/shader variation; it is **not** automatically
cross-API. Exact visual equivalence depends on the host's G-buffer,
triangle transforms, and frame ordering.
